    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer; all devices participate in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the expert that the router selected.\n\nThese are orthogonal and frequently combined: TP shards dense attention/MLP blocks, while EP shards the MoE feed-forward layers. This note covers the mechanics of both, their data flow, the collectives they rely on, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with attention and an MLP block. The canonical Megatron-style layout for TP degree *T*:\n\n**Attention:**\n- Query, Key, Value projections `W_q, W_k, W_v` are **column-parallel**: each rank holds `W \u2208 R^{d_model \u00d7 (d_head\u00b7n_heads/T)}`. Heads are partitioned across ranks, so each rank computes attention for its own heads independently \u2014 no communication needed inside attention.\n- Output projection `W_o` is **row-parallel**: each rank holds a slice of the input dimension. Each rank produces a *partial* sum of the output.\n\n**MLP:**\n- First GEMM `W_up` (and gate projection in SwiGLU) is column-parallel.\n- Second GEMM `W_down` is row-parallel, again producing partial outputs.\n\n**Embeddings and LM head** are typically vocabulary-parallel",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
